      "unique_scope_text": "Monomial and Clifford constructions\n\nThe first part of this research companion records the direct odd-prime and weighted dyadic construc-\ntions, including the low-rank carrier repairs. This continuation returns to the earlier monomial and\nClifford branches. Their conclusions are not interchangeable. A genuine arithmetic candidate may have\nthe right dimension and conductor while failing to contain a prescribed full simple character. A finite\ncarrier may have enough dimension while failing the required action or marking. A compatible formal\ninterface may still await its actual arithmetic input.\n\nThe purpose here is to make those distinctions mathematically usable. We extract the precise restricted\nimplications, explain the negative results, and retain the surviving routes. The presentation includes\nelementary proofs where the argument is short enough to expose completely. Deeper arithmetic state-\nments are identified as inherited ordinary arguments with named classical inputs. No theorem in this\ncontinuation is newly certified by Lean.\n\nSource provenance. The first part of this companion is retained from a preserved reader. Its complete\neditable source archive was not recovered. The monomial and Clifford analysis uses the retained\ninitial research archive. The source map records this boundary. This book does not claim to\nreconstruct unavailable source files.\n\n\nMathematical scope. Conjecture 66 remains the general arithmetic realization obligation, with the\nsame-candidate H5 comparison kept separate. The positive constructions below are restricted statements.\nThe negative examples refute specified shortcuts or prescribed candidates, not the entire conjecture."
    },
    {
      "page_1based": 56,
      "kept_by_candidate": true,
      "no_Contents_heading": true,
      "outside_edit_words_and_bboxes_exact": true,
