      "max_bbox_drift_pt": 0.0,
      "unique_scope_text": "Conductors and native markings\n\nA conductor is an arithmetic invariant, not a complete description of a type. A nondegenerate commuta-\ntor is a finite invariant, not a complete description of a marked dyadic extension. These two distinctions\nmeet at the smallest dyadic construction with exactly enough quotient capacity. There is no spare plane\nthere to absorb an independently prescribed quadratic mismatch.\n\nThis continuation audits the conductor and marking branches at their actual hypotheses. It keeps three\narithmetic objects separate: the original source representation, a candidate obtained through a classical\ninduction and descent construction, and a candidate arising from a proposed explicit type. A compari-\nson is useful only when its normalization and its subject are fixed.\n\nThe resulting finite theory is also constructive. A discrepancy between two quadratic refinements be-\ncomes one linear functional. When its Arf obstruction vanishes, a single transvection gives an explicit\ncorrection. For a supplied odd-order finite action, a further central discrepancy can be removed by a\nfinite sum. Neither correction constructs the missing arithmetic action, chooses a canonical native mark-\ning, or settles the full phase equations.\n\nSource provenance. The conductor and marking analysis retains the earlier parts’ source and review\nboundaries. In particular, the first part is available as a preserved reader, while its complete editable\narchive remains unavailable. The evidence ledger distinguishes this documentary gap from the\nunresolved mathematical obligations.\n\nEvidence status. Ordinary finite proofs are supplied below. The arithmetic construction records are\ninherited arguments audited against selected primary statements. Recorded exact tests check finite\nmodels and normalizations. No fresh Lean compilation or external peer review is claimed."
    }
