      "orientation_text": "A Visual atlas\n\nThe plates give exact finite models for the algebraic constructions in this volume. Their stable labels\nrun from Plate III.1 to Plate III.24. Each plate names the related statements in the chapter. These models\nmake the hypotheses and map directions visible. They do not extend the finite Lean contract to the\nanalytic results of Part II.\n\n\n   Three routes through the atlas\n\n  For typed transfer, read Plate III.3 to Plate III.8. For coordinates, connections, and gauge, read Plate\n   III.9 to Plate III.18, followed by Plate III.23. For candidate witnesses and trees, read Plate III.19 to\n   Plate III.22, followed by Plate III.24. Plate III.1 and Plate III.2 introduce the objects and the questions\n  they answer.\n\n  Products act in traversal order. The first edge traversed is the rightmost operator. Rows represent\ntargets and columns represent sources.",
      "canonical_visual_opening_text": "  How to use this volume\n\n  Twenty-four worked plates and twenty-two exact model figures accompany the monograph and\n   its Lean guide. Integer, rational, and symbolic calculations determine the figures. The source pack-\n  age includes the model generators and their explicit finite verification ranges.\n\n\n A route through the examples\n\n  The labels Plate III.1 through Plate III.24 identify worked pages independently of PDF pagination.\nEach page names its corresponding statement in the main text. The Lean guide provides the separate\nformal contract.\n\n\nPlate III.1 Three objects, three different questions       Plate III.13 The identity can hide in the spectrum\n\nPlate III.2 An atlas of the objects                       Plate III.14 Gauge changes the picture, not flatness\n\nPlate III.3 Read the product in execution order          Plate III.15 Flat is not the same as calibrated\n\nPlate III.4 All sixteen words, only five walks            Plate III.16 Resolve the entire obstruction\n\nPlate III.5 Coefficients need not commute               Plate III.17 Why diagonal checks are insufficient\n\nPlate III.6 A reachable packet can receive zero          Plate III.18 A gauge can move every packet label\n\nPlate III.7 Reachability is only an upper bound         Plate III.19 An obstruction rejects one candidate\n\nPlate III.8 Paths through changing packet lists          Plate III.20 A tree reads its named leaves\n\nPlate III.9 One vector, three exact frames               Plate III.21 Where a nonlinear defect can enter\n\nPlate III.10 Nonlinear charts still cancel exactly         Plate III.22 Mixed terms are not optional\n\nPlate III.11 Reversibility is a real hypothesis            Plate III.23 From loop data to a packet witness\n\nPlate III.12 Measure the mismatch at its base           Plate III.24 Read the evidence with the pictures\n\n\n\n  Three reading routes\n\n  Typed transfer: Plate III.3 to Plate III.8. Coordinates, connection, and gauge: Plate III.9 to Plate III.18,\nthen Plate III.23. Candidate witnesses and trees: Plate III.19 to Plate III.22, then Plate III.24.\n\n  From the finite models to the broader chapter\n\n  The monograph continues with rooted tree expansions, nonlinear refinement defects, obstruction\nobjects, filtered lifting, elimination, and smooth projector transport. Read the finite examples as con-\ncrete preparation. The broader ordinary proofs keep their own hypotheses and are distinct from the\nfinite Lean contract. Chapter III: rooted trees: Section 18. Nonlinear defects: Section 20. Obstruction objects:\nSection 21. Filtered lifting: Section 23. Smooth transport: Section 27.\n\n  Read products in traversal order\n A path 𝑒1, 𝑒2, … , 𝑒𝑛acts as 𝐿𝑒𝑛⋯𝐿𝑒2𝐿𝑒1: the first traversed edge is the rightmost operator. Row in-\ndices are targets and column indices are sources throughout the guide."
